{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "An array gives you four basic operations. Two of them are effectively free, and two of them get more expensive as the array grows. Knowing which is which is most of what separates a fast solution from a slow one."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Reading and writing a slot"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Reading `ages[3]` or assigning `ages[3] = 22` takes the same amount of time regardless of whether the array holds ten values or ten million. The machine computes where slot 3 lives and goes straight there — it never looks at the other slots."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "const third = ages[3];   // read  — instant\nages[3] = 22;            // write — instant"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "We write this cost as **O(1)**, meaning the time does not depend on the size of the array. This is the array's defining superpower, and the next lesson shows exactly how it is achieved."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Inserting and deleting"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Now the expensive pair. An array is one unbroken run of slots with no gaps, and that rule has to hold *after* your change as well as before it. So inserting into the middle means making room."
    },
    {
      "type": "drawing",
      "src": "/drawings/6d53ac9d-91a1-4afc-a97f-231e61c24de7.svg",
      "alt": "Inserting a value in the middle of an array shifts every later element one slot right",
      "caption": "To insert at index 2, everything from index 2 onward must shuffle right",
      "width": 900,
      "height": 320,
