    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "To insert at index 2, every element from index 2 onward has to shift one slot to the right — and the work grows with how many elements sit to the right of your insertion point. Deleting has the mirror problem: remove a middle element and everything after it must shift left to close the gap."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "In the worst case you touch nearly every element, so insertion and deletion cost **O(N)**, where N is the number of elements. Note the exception worth remembering: inserting or deleting at the **end** shifts nothing, so it is O(1)."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Searching"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "If you know the index, finding a value is instant. If you only know the value, you have no choice but to look — checking slot 0, then 1, then 2, until you find it or run out of array."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "That is **O(N)** in the worst case. There is a large exception: if the array is **sorted**, binary search finds any value in O(log N), which is dramatically faster. That technique gets its own chapter later in this course."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The whole picture"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**Read or write by index** — O(1). Free, whatever the size.",
        "**Insert or delete at the end** — O(1). Nothing needs to move.",
        "**Insert or delete in the middle** — O(N). Everything after the change shifts.",
        "**Search for a value** — O(N) unsorted, O(log N) if sorted."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Read that list as a description of what arrays are *for*. They are superb when you mostly read by index and append at the end, and a poor fit when you are constantly inserting into the middle. When a problem is dominated by middle insertions, that is the signal to reach for a linked list instead — which is exactly where this course goes next."
    }
  ],
